\chapter{Nominal records and generic enums}
\label{ch:23}
\index{Nominal records and generic enums}

Two named records can share field names and field types yet retain different identities. The compiler associates a nominal value with its declaration, including module identity and any concrete type arguments. Generic enums are instantiated according to their argument list. Construct a value against the intended nominal type and match on constructors of that specific enum.

\OnxInputCode[firstline=1,lastline=19]{../../on1x/examples/nominal_adts.on1x}
A declaration graph must support a finite layout. By-value declaration cycles are rejected. Copy-safe nominal layouts can use the direct C11 representation. Immutable shared data and recursive unique owning layouts extend it under their own restrictions in \cref{ch:25} and \cref{ch:26}. When debugging a mismatch, compare the resolved module and type arguments as well as the human-readable fields. Two aliases of the same module refer back to its declaration identity. Two independently declared but similarly spelled enums do not become interchangeable by having the same constructors.

\section{Infer an argument when there is no payload}
The fixture makes a point whose field initializers appear in the order
y then x. The magnitude function in this example \emph{adds} the integer
fields: two plus forty is 42. Constructing Some with that result gives
the compiler evidence for the missing argument of Option: it must be an
integer. The fallback supplied to the chooser must have that type as
well, since either the payload or the fallback can be returned. Here
Some wins, so the number printed is 42.

In the next call the None constructor carries only the empty record.
There is no Boolean stored in it for the checker to find. Instead
the fallback is true. Since the chooser returns that fallback for None
and the payload for Some, the fallback constrains the missing type
argument to Boolean. That call prints true. Leaving a hole in a type
argument thus asks for constraints from the entire use, not just from
the fields present in the active constructor.

Finally the error example uses two type arguments. The error arm
extracts an unsigned byte, converts seven to a signed integer, and
subtracts it from zero. It prints minus seven. The conversion is
necessary for the arithmetic; matching an enum identifies its payload
but does not implicitly widen it to the function's return type.

